  <datafield tag="590" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Getty's copy is no. 22.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Consists of six necktie-shaped sections of black paper, folded accordion fashion. Each section is silkscreened with the necktie pattern of a British colonial regiment. One or two stripes from each tie have been cut out to reveal a white paper slide. When pulled upwards, the slides are found to be printed with poems that refer to colonial trade with six regions of the world.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">The versos of the six necktie-shaped sections have areas of white paper (ca. 2 x 5 cm.), intended to recall manufacturers' labels on the back of ties. The two outer "labels" are printed with title, author and imprint. The remaining four are windows cut through the black paper to reveal the white paper slides within, printed with the names of four raw materials in colonial trade: RUBBER / SILK / COTTON / COPPER. When the slides are pulled upwards, additional words form a colophon for the present publication: RUBBER stamp and / SILK screen on / COTTON rag paper with / COPPER wire and raffia binding.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">In a silk-screened envelope of black paper (42 x 13 cm.) designed to resemble a store's enclosure for a necktie. On the inside of the flap at top is a swatch of striped silk repp.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">"Edition of 30, plus A-L lettered series for non-commercial use"--Artist's accompanying description.</subfield>
  </datafield>
